BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State and derive Nyquist's Maximum Bitrate Formula for noiseless channels and Shannon's Channel Capacity Theorem for noisy channels. A communications link has bandwidth B = 4 kHz and Signal-to-Noise Ratio SNR = 3162. Compute the theoretical Shannon capacity.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Nyquist Bitrate Theorem \(Noiseless Channel\):) Tj
T*
(   Harry Nyquist \(1928\) demonstrated that passing an analog signal through a low-pass filter of) Tj
T*
(   bandwidth B limits the maximum independent symbol rate to 2*B baud without Inter-Symbol) Tj
T*
(   Interference \(ISI\).) Tj
T*
(   If each signal symbol is quantized into M distinct discrete voltage levels, each symbol encodes) Tj
T*
(   log2\(M\) bits.) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: Bitrate_max = 2 * B * log2\(M\) bits per second.) Tj
T*
(   - Implication: In a hypothetical noiseless channel, bitrate can be increased indefinitely by) Tj
T*
(   increasing signal levels M \(limited in practice only by receiver voltage sensitivity\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Shannon Channel Capacity Theorem \(Noisy Channel\):) Tj
T*
(   Claude Shannon \(1948\) proved that in any real physical channel contaminated by Additive White) Tj
T*
(   Gaussian Noise \(AWGN\), the maximum theoretical error-free data rate C is strictly bounded) Tj
T*
(   regardless of modulation levels:) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: C = B * log2\( 1 + SNR \) bits per second,) Tj
T*
(   where B is channel bandwidth in Hertz, and SNR = P_signal / P_noise is the linear) Tj
T*
(   signal-to-noise ratio.) Tj
T*
(   - In decibels: SNR_dB = 10 * log10\( SNR \) => SNR = 10^\( SNR_dB / 10 \).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Numerical Problem Solving:) Tj
T*
(   - Given data: Bandwidth B = 4 kHz = 4,000 Hz; Linear SNR = 3162 \(corresponding to SNR_dB =) Tj
T*
(   10*log10\(3162\) = 35 dB\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: Calculate \(1 + SNR\) = 1 + 3162 = 3163.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: Compute log2\(3163\) using change of base: log2\(3163\) = ln\(3163\) / ln\(2\) = 8.0593 /) Tj
T*
(   0.69315 = 11.627 bits.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: Compute Capacity C = B * log2\(1 + SNR\) = 4,000 * 11.627 = 46,508 bps =~ 46.51 kbps.) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion: The absolute physical upper bound for error-free transmission across this channel) Tj
T*
(   is 46.51 kbps.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
